# tools/al/ales_stereo_trim_silence_witness.rish -- stereo_trim_silence: the silence stripper carried into stereo, two independent silences cut in one lockstep, the suite's first stereo edit built on the segmenter (ALES148).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus - the creative suite) - waymark ALES - rung ALES148.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-051802_fill-ales148-lotus-stereo-trim-silence.md
# Stands on: lotus/stereo_segment.rye (ALES147 -- the stereo voice-activity segmenter, run once to read BOTH channels' silences over one shared frame grid, its segment_stereo, its StereoSegments, its StereoSegmentError, reused
# whole), lotus/segment.rye (ALES95 -- the mono segmenter and max_segments, through ALES147), lotus/trim_silence.rye (ALES96 -- the mono silence stripper, imported by the selftest to prove the lockstep law), lotus/pan.rye
# (ALES10 -- the StereoClip, two mono Clips left/right, its balance invariant), lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2 -- the Clip, its cut, its max_clip, the EditError reused whole), lotus/voiced.rye (ALES94 -- the .silent verdict this
# intersects). A Lotus master is a StereoClip whose defining invariant is that left and right hold the same length; ALES147 proved the two channels segment independently, so their silences differ in count and boundary, and
# trimming each channel by its own silence would tear the stereo image apart.
#
# lotus/stereo_trim_silence.rye decides ONE cut for both channels -- the silence intersection: segment both channels over one shared frame grid, mark a frame jointly-silent only where it is silent in both channels, and cut
# exactly those frames from both channels in lockstep (back to front, so every earlier frame's index stays valid). A frame silent in only one channel is kept -- content in either speaker survives; only a moment where neither
# speaker carries sound is removed. It validates the shared frame length, band, span, and floor once through ALES147's own up-front checks before any cut (BadThreshold on an illegal band, BadRange on a span past the samples,
# BadFloor on a floor above the peak ceiling, BadFrame on a zero/oversized/non-dividing length), so a refusal never trims one channel and leaves the other; StereoSegmentError and EditError reused whole. The crux is that it
# invents no new measurement and no new sample -- it reads its silences through ALES147 and removes whole frames through ALES2's cut -- so it can never drift from the segmenter it composes; what is new is the composition rule,
# the intersection of two independent silences, whose whole honesty is that running the proven mono trim_silence on each channel apart would desync the pair.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/al/ales_stereo_trim_silence_witness.rish

say "ales-stereo-trim-silence: Language EN — stereo_trim_silence removes every jointly-silent frame from both channels of a stereo master, leaving the audio contiguous and the stereo image balanced. It segments both channels of the whole clip into frames of one length through ALES147's stereo segmenter, marks a frame jointly-silent only where it is silent in both channels, and cuts exactly those frames from both channels through ALES2's cut, walking back to front so every earlier frame's index stays valid. A Lotus master is two Clips heard together, one per speaker, whose defining invariant is that left and right hold the same length; a frame silent in only one channel is kept, because the other speaker still carries sound, and only a moment where neither speaker carries sound is dead air. It validates the shared frame length, band, span, and floor once before any cut so a refusal never trims one channel and leaves the other."
say "ales-stereo-trim-silence: Style Radiant — ALES96 trims a mono clip by cutting its silent runs, the dead-air remover every recorder ships. Carried into stereo it meets the wall the length-preserving edits never did: ALES147 proved the two channels segment independently, their silences differing in count and boundary, so trimming each channel by its own silence would give the two speakers different lengths and tear the stereo image apart. This rung cannot run the mono trimmer twice; it decides one cut that serves both — the silence intersection: cut only the frames silent in both channels, keep every frame either speaker fills. A sound panned hard to the left is real audio the right's silence must never delete. The crux is that it invents no new measurement and no new sample — it reads its silences through ALES147, which reads them through ALES95, ALES94, and the two readers those fuse, and removes whole frames through ALES2's cut, so it can never drift from the segmenter it composes. What is new is the composition rule, the intersection of two independent silences, and its whole honesty is that running the proven mono trim_silence on each channel apart would desync the pair, where this cuts once for both and keeps them balanced: the first stereo edit that had to think, and thought the minimal honest thought. The crux stands out as four laws — the stereo trim law (the result equals a by-hand keep of the frames not jointly-silent, per channel, across many frame lengths, floors, and splits, both channels leaving equal length), the preservation law (a frame quiet on the left yet loud on the right kept on both, only the jointly-silent frame removed and its gap closed), the lockstep law (per-channel mono trim desyncing to lengths eight and twelve while the stereo trim holds both at twelve), and the atomicity and degenerate laws (an all-jointly-silent master trimming to empty on both, a master loud on at least one channel per frame unchanged on both, an empty clip and a ragged frame length and an inverted band and an illegal floor each refusing by name with both channels untouched and still balanced)."
say "ales-stereo-trim-silence: Lens TAME — two bounded i16 Clips (left, right) trimmed in lockstep by one shared frame length, band, floor, and split; the balance invariant is asserted on entry and exit, and all validation runs once up front through ALES147's segment_stereo (ALES92's precheck for band and span, the floor against meter's sample_peak_max, the frame-length divisibility) read-only before a single sample is cut, so a refusal leaves both channels untouched; the forward pass records a bounded jointly-silent frame mask (sized once at max_clip, one bool per frame) by advancing one monotonic cursor per channel across its own tiling run list and marking a frame only where it is silent in both, asserting each cursor names the run that covers the frame start and that the removed samples never exceed the clip; the backward pass cuts each jointly-silent frame from both channels through ALES2's cut with the in-bounds span asserted before each cut so neither can fault and leave the pair half-trimmed, and the postcondition asserts both channels lost exactly the jointly-silent samples and leave equal length; the stereo trim law is proven against an independent by-hand reference (a second implementation reading the frame verdicts by run scan) across five frame lengths, four floors, and three splits, the preservation law proves a left-silent right-loud frame is kept on both, the lockstep law proves the mono trimmer desyncs where this does not, and the degenerate and atomicity cases leave both channels untouched and balanced; StereoSegmentError and EditError reused whole, siloed to lotus/, edits only by removing whole frames, fabricating no samples and reordering nothing, no adaptive floor, no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/stereo_trim_silence"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/stereo_trim_silence.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-stereo-trim-silence: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/stereo_trim_silence.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-stereo-trim-silence: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-stereo-trim-silence: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-stereo-trim-silence' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-stereo-trim-silence: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-stereo-trim-silence line"
